1. INTERVIEW READY ANSWERS
======================================================================

Q1. What is Searching?

Answer:
Searching is the process of finding a required element (key) from a collection of data. It returns the index of the element if found; otherwise, it returns -1 (or indicates that the element is not present).

Types:
• Linear Search
• Binary Search

------------------------------------------------------------

Q2. What is Linear Search?

Answer:
Linear Search checks each element sequentially from the beginning until the target element is found or the array ends.

Best Case
→ O(1)

Worst Case
→ O(n)

Space Complexity
→ O(1)

------------------------------------------------------------

Q3. Advantages of Linear Search

Answer:
• Works on sorted and unsorted data.
• Very easy to implement.
• No preprocessing required.

------------------------------------------------------------

Q4. Disadvantages of Linear Search

Answer:
• Slow for large datasets.
• May need to scan the entire array.
• Worst-case time complexity is O(n).

------------------------------------------------------------

Q5. Why can't Binary Search work on an unsorted array?

Answer:
Binary Search decides whether to search the left half or the right half based on the middle element. This decision is valid only when the array is sorted. In an unsorted array, discarding half of the elements may remove the target itself, leading to incorrect results.

------------------------------------------------------------

Q6. What is Binary Search?

Answer:
Binary Search is an efficient searching algorithm used only on sorted arrays. It repeatedly compares the target with the middle element and discards half of the search space in every iteration.

Working

1. Initialize
   left = 0
   right = n-1

2. Find
   mid = (left + right) / 2

3. Compare

arr[mid] == key
→ Found

arr[mid] < key
→ left = mid + 1

arr[mid] > key
→ right = mid - 1

Repeat until found or left > right.

------------------------------------------------------------

Q7. Why is Binary Search O(log n)?

Answer:
In every iteration, Binary Search removes half of the remaining search space.

Search Space

n
↓

n/2
↓

n/4
↓

n/8
↓

...

After k iterations

n / 2^k = 1

Therefore,

k = log₂n

Hence,

Time Complexity = O(log n)

------------------------------------------------------------

Q8. Linear Search vs Binary Search

Feature              Linear Search            Binary Search

Requirement          Sorted/Unsorted          Sorted only

Working              Sequential               Divide into halves

Best Time            O(1)                    O(1)

Worst Time           O(n)                    O(log n)

Space                O(1)                    O(1) (Iterative)

Use Case             Small/Unsorted          Large Sorted Data

